Frequently Asked Questions
What is dangerouslySetInnerHTML in React?
dangerouslySetInnerHTML is React's replacement for the browser DOM innerHTML property. It accepts an object with a __html key containing the HTML string to render. React intentionally gave it a scary name to remind developers that injecting raw HTML carries XSS risks.
Why is it called dangerouslySetInnerHTML?
The name is intentionally alarming to make developers pause and consider the security implications. Injecting unsanitized HTML can lead to cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks where malicious code executes in users' browsers, potentially stealing cookies, session tokens, or personal data.
How do I sanitize HTML before using dangerouslySetInnerHTML?
Use DOMPurify: import DOMPurify from 'dompurify' and wrap your HTML with DOMPurify.sanitize(htmlString). This removes script tags, event handlers, and other dangerous elements while preserving safe formatting tags like p, strong, em, and a.
What are alternatives to dangerouslySetInnerHTML?
html-react-parser converts HTML strings to React elements without innerHTML. react-markdown renders Markdown safely. For simple cases, you can manually parse HTML server-side and pass structured data to React components instead of raw HTML strings.
